A||||95||Cheever3000||Let's talk about FOCOA||||2||
Z||000000||Cheever3000||12-12-2001||02:45 AM||||I never wanted to publicly put down FOCOA.  If it came up in conversation, I just said it wasn’t for me.  As I helped start a new international club, we vowed we would not proclaim it to be a replacement for FOCOA, rather an alternative.  But when I’m asked what are the benefits of CFOG-I membership, I have to wonder: what are the benefits of FOCOA membership?<P>Let's take a look at FOCOA and find out why it's so great.  Let’s check into its qualifications for claiming to represent all Fiero owners everywhere. Please consider the following, as you read their own website at <A HREF="http://www.fieroownersclub.com/phil.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroownersclub.com/phil.htm</A> and a little history lesson they provide at <A HREF="http://www.flash.net/~newboy/foa.html." TARGET=_blank>http://www.flash.net/~newboy/foa.html.</A>  <P>It is supposedly comprised of 8 regions.  Do you know what those regions are? I could never get the answer.  Can you name the regional directors?  I have only heard from 3 of them.  Why are those basic facts so hard to uncover?  Can you contact the directors, by email or phone, and get a response?  <P>Is FOCOA "member run" as they claim?  Are they "keeping Fiero drivers informed of what is happening within the Fiero community"?  Are they "sharing information of all kinds"?  Is it a club that is "responsive to its members"?  Does it maintain its website?  I guess by now everyone knows about how they let a porn site take over the former URL.  The new website still invites you to a national show that took place last summer!  Yet the home page boasts: "Browse this site for the latest in Fiero happenings"!  Do they involve members in decisions?  Were elections held in 2000 for four of the eight directors, as promised?  Do they "unite the community into a single-minded, member-run organization"?  Do they have open-book finances?  How many people have paid for, and never received, their 4 magazines per year, or their collection of back issues?  Do they recognize and encourage participation in POCI or PFF?  What have they done lately to promote the joys of Fiero ownership to those who don't already know? <P>According to my observations, and conversations with FOCOA members, you probably will not get from FOCOA: (1) information; (2) communication; (3) involvement; (4) representation; and (5) what they promise.  But you will get a renewal notice, you will get an invitation to one national show each year (to which Phil Huff does not even drive his own Fiero), and if you're very lucky, you might eventually get some magazines. <P>So you tell me if you get those benefits out of your membership with FOCOA.  You tell me if they justify their representation of all Fiero owners everywhere.  Are they constantly improving and progressing?  How much fun was the Williamsburg show last summer?  Have any trouble finding a restroom there?  And they've been doing these shows for how long?  If you're an autocrosser, do you see any news about other Fieros in your sport? When was the last time the website was updated?  Answer: 6 months ago! <P>I'm sorry if all this sounds rude, but it's so sad what the potential was, and what the intentions were, but then they didn't have what it took to make it happen.  That's because it has been a one-man show.  And I know Phil Huff is a nice guy, cause everybody says so.  But what he wants to do and what he is able to do are not the same thing.  And you can't successfully run both a parts business and a national organization.  The bottom line is you have to work hard if you claim to represent the Fiero community!  CFOG-I is working hard, and will continue to do so.  We will unite and represent.  We will inform, communicate, and involve the membership.  We don't take our promises lightly.  We will be directed by you the Fiero owner, not only through your suggestions, but by holding elections for every position of the organization from top to bottom.  Now, tell me why that kind of Fiero club cannot succeed.  Please check out <A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org." TARGET=_blank>http://www.cfog-i.org.</A>   <P>Chuck Hodges, Vice President<P>Dale Starcher, Central Region Director <BR>dalelee22@cs.com<p>[This message has been edited by Cheever3000 (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||2
Z||000001||LZeitgeist||12-12-2001||08:41 AM||||FOCOA *is* "run by its members". In other words, send them a check, and then do whatever you were going to do anyway, only poorer with nothing to show for it. <P>I'm glad I never fell into that fiasco... I met Phil Huff** at Williamsburg, and he seems like a really nice guy - had a couple really enjoyable conversations with him over the course of the weekend, and when Formula88 and I were browsing around the "store", he was literally stuffing freebies in our pockets (stuff we didn't even know was free for the taking)... however, I'd rather invest the membership money directly in my Fiero, because then I *know* I (and my car) are better off. <P>Although I'd love for a national club to exist, I can't afford to travel much, so I'd end up missing 95% of the national gatherings anyway... so I prefer local chapters. However, I've belonged to enough local 'fan clubs' and stuff that I've learned when money and memberships are involved, politics and wasteful BS usually follow closely behind unless *really* tight control is held over things, which just means more work is involved in the whole process.<P>Here in North Carolina, we have the completely unofficial group known as "Them Yahoos in North Carolina What With the Fieros" <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">, or sometimes the group is erroneously called "Tarheel Fieros".  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> (Just kidding, Bob.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) The group is nothing more than an e-mail list led by one person... no dues, no paperwork, no membership... and we all get together (in varying numbers) every month somewhere across the state for a lunch and a Fiero showing. It's a cool reason to wash up the car, get out of the house and have some fun for a few hours. The only work involved (that I'm aware of) is choosing a place to eat in one of the three areas across the state (Raleigh vicinity, Greensboro vicinity, and Charlotte vicinity, which means that in the worst-case scenario, every third month there is a gathering pretty close to where you are) and making sure accurate directions are sent to Bob to give to the listmembers. (I still want to try again for that Kitty Hawk rendezvous with the Virgina Fiero club... I feel bad that things turned out as poorly as they did last time.)<P>I guess what I'm getting at is that a club's going to have to have some track record and history before I'd be willing to sign my name (and my money) up... I know that doesn't help the start-up of a new organization, but if I can't (or won't be willing to put forth the effort and money to) go to the national-level happenings, then there's little reason for me to stick my neck (and my wallet) out, when the two things I enjoy most from "membership" are making friends and exchanging information - and both of those are already here at Pennock's in abundance. <P>I emotionally support the efforts to start a new national club, and I hope it will become something glorious. But for now, that's the best I can do... <P>Good luck with the whole endeavor.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>**not to be confused with Phil Randolph (a.k.a. 'Phil' here at Pennock's), who uses poor, innocent, small woodland creatures in terrible ways to make his car go faster.. tsk, tsk, tsk... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)<P>------------------<BR>Patrick W. Heinske  --  LZeitgeist@aol.com<BR>1988 Red Fiero Formula<BR>- 1st Place - Stock Coupe - FOCOSEVA 2000<BR>- 3rd Place - Stock Formula - FOCOA Nat'l 2001<BR>- Class Winner - 1982 to Present - Tarheel Tigers All-Pontiac Show 2001||||reg||1
Z||000002||2M4 Dale||12-12-2001||10:09 AM||||Patrick.....watch us and see !!!<P>Pennocks is great !!!<BR>FOCOA could've been<BR>CFOG-I  will be !!!<P>c'mon guys lets have some input here.......one of the reasons we got this "new club" thing going in the first place is because of all the complaints we've heard from PFF members||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||12-12-2001||10:09 AM||||Well, the Williamsburg show was my first FOCOA Fiero show.  I paid $50 to enter the show, and another $25 for the banquet.  The banquet was nice, no complaints there.  But for my $50 I got the priveledge to park in a school parking lot one morning for the "show."  A show that had food vendors, but no rest room facilities.  A show that had us list our cars based on a very complicated rules process, only to have it changed at the last minute - without notice.  For example, my car was in Stock, Formula, Unrestored, with 60-120K miles.  When the judging took place, that became the "Formula" class.  No distinctions other than they had to be stock, or they went into Semi-mod or Full-mod classes.<P>Phil Huff seems to be a great guy with a genuine love of the Fiero.  Unfortunately, one man cannot run a national organization alone.  And it appears that's what he has to do.<P>FOCOA has been doing shows for how long?  I went to a Pontiac show in Raleigh earlier this year.  Granted, it was smaller, but it was their first show ever - and the trophies were higher quality, the show was better run, and the entry fee was less.  I would think that after almost 20 years, all the little things that go into making a show run smoothly would be second nature and not a big fiasco.||||reg||4
Z||000004||BOILERMAKER||12-12-2001||10:26 AM||||This group is going to be great.  We have a lot planned, and are working feverishly on setting things up.  We are making great strides towards a solid ground and are getting ready to send out our first newsletter.  The potential is there for a great organization and I support it 100%. I know that the staff we have will be more than capable of setting this club above all others.<P>Sure there will be skeptics along the way, but we can't (and won't) forget the owners and why we put this club together in the first place.||||reg||14
Z||000005||LZeitgeist||12-12-2001||10:43 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Patrick.....watch us and see !!!<P>Pennocks is great !!!<BR>FOCOA could've been<BR>CFOG-I will be !!!<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Yup, I'm a-watchin'!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>And hell, I've done my thing to help causes that weren't even as worthy as this, so I'm not saying I won't ever help... mostly that financially I can't help right at this moment, and support-wise I've gotten burned before, so I'm a little more cautious now where I aim my 'enthusiasm gun'... but I have faith and trust in the people I've met here, and so I imagine I'll get more involved sooner or later.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Whoohoo! 8)||||reg||1
Z||000006||CoolBlue87GT||12-12-2001||10:47 AM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cheever3000:<BR><B>as you read their own website at <A HREF="http://www.fieroownersclub.com," TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroownersclub.com,</A>  and a little history lesson they provide at <A HREF="http://www.flash.net/~newboy/foa.html." TARGET=_blank>http://www.flash.net/~newboy/foa.html.</A> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It's funny you bring this up, just yesterday I was looking at their web site, and remembering the Williamsburg show.<BR>(b.t.w. the paths you supplied, didn't work when I tried them)<P>Try this one, <A HREF="http://www.fieroownersclub.com/phil.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroownersclub.com/phil.htm</A> <P>I've talked w/ Phil by phone & at the show. From what I've seen, I do like him.<P>Not having the bathrooms at the show, well - chalk that up to an opps.  I did notice that.<P>Setting up a show from so many states away, that was an error, but not going to nail him for that.<P>The trophy's, - yes, they could have been a better. Look like they were printed on a computer printer. <P>BUT, I did enjoy the show, - because of all the people I met, and friends I maded on those two days.<P>You brought up some points that I can't agree / nor disagree with, as I don't know that much about what you have said.<P>I only know Phil from the show.<P>I hope both groups can exist without attacking each other.<BR>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/CoolBluesig.gif">   <BR><p>[This message has been edited by CoolBlue87GT (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000007||LZeitgeist||12-12-2001||11:06 AM||||To piggyback on Formula88's post - yeah, I was mightily pi$$ed on his behalf about the car classifications... we spent a good couple hours figuring out exactly which classes our cars (mine, his, and Dad's, but Dad ended up not being able to go) should be in so as to not cause problems at registration, and we were also pleased to see that we would be in different classes due to the mileage differences on our otherwise (pretty much) identical Formulas. Hey, who knows - maybe we'd both bring home a first in our respective classes... that'd be cool as heck!<P>And even as the cars were being lined up, and we counted (I think) 13 Formulas, they were *still* showing the different class information, so we felt that we were safe and right. (One caveat - there were a couple things that Formula88 was working on - his sail panels, for example - that he *knew* were going to cost him points. But we figured that if there were only three people in his *specific* class, he'd still go home with a trophy - his first ever in a car show.) However, when, out of the blue, with *absolutely* <B>NO</B> notice whatsoever, they lumped all of the Formulas in the same group. Boom. Just like that. No notice, no previous announcement, no mention of the possibility at registration that "if we only get X number of entries, the classes will be combined" - nothing. To top it off, at this stage in the game, they change the rules <B>WELL</B> after they've got your money and put you through the entire process and never even mentioned the possibility of refunds connected to the rules being changed or anything else. I know Formula88 was pretty irritated, but<B>I WAS PI$$ED.</B> If we'd have known that the class was going to be combined, being that this was a national show, he could have saved his $50 since we knew there were things about his car that he hadn't had the time/money to finish yet that would knock him out of the top three.<P>I don't know how much input Curry Obenshain had into that particular decision, or if it was completely Phil Huff's - but it was <B>WRONG</B>. Perhaps not the decision - I can see the reason *why* they combined the cars into one class - but the way they did it was wrong, wrong, wrong. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||LZeitgeist||12-12-2001||11:06 AM||||EDIT - double post.<p>[This message has been edited by LZeitgeist (edited 12-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||2M4 Dale||12-12-2001||11:16 AM||||Yup...Phil's a nice guy. I met him at the Louisville Festival in 2000. <P>That being besides the point. We need a club that is responsive to its membership !!!<P>We don't need to fight within the ranks of Fierodom......we do need continuity and accountability !!!||||reg||1
Z||000010||webbee||12-12-2001||11:56 AM||||Seems to me that we already have an international club that works really well,<BR>right here at Pennocks Fiero Forum.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Voytek||12-12-2001||12:24 PM||||I've received an e-mail inviting me to join the CFOG-I the other day.  Apparently this is to be a truly international club.  The idea sounds good and I'm interested, BUT, generally, with these big-time clubs, event costs are high and involvement is low.<P>There is an international car club (DSCR or something) that a number of us (from our local Fiero club - CFOG, BTW - Calgary Fiero Owners Group) have joined.  Well, let's use this example.  When our Fiero club meets at the track, we pay the track fee and have fun.  When DSCR meets at the track, everyone has to dish out an additional $20 bucks for the club fee.  Needless to say, I haven't gone to any get-togethers.  Why would I want to pay more?<P>Here's another concern: I've noticed that some key officials from CFOG-I are from the Fiero Shop in California.  While there is nothing wrong with California, I've seen a substantial number of complaints about the Fiero Shop here on PFF.  I've also tried getting advice from the FS on installing a 3.1 in my GT and received none.<P>In light of the above, how can anyone convince me (or others) to join the club and that things will be different?  It's great that it is an international effort and we already have a couple of reps in Canada (none in Western Canada yet) and very encouraging to see so much involvement right from the start, but I need more.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||tgowens||12-12-2001||12:38 PM||||Voytek,<P>Three things I would like to say to you here about your post........<P>1. I agree and understand about paying fees for an event only to have to turn around and pay another fee to join a club. There <I>is</I> a membership fee associated with the joining of CFOG-International. This membership fee is quite small when you realize that those funds will be used to provide the club magazine, merchandising which may be available, administrative costs such as mailing and postage fees, web site cost, telephone costs, etc ........ Any organization of this type will have to have a budget to work with, unless it is subsidized by a business, which this one is not. But, there <I>will</I> be reduced fees for its members at CFOG-International shows as opposed to the fees a non-member attendee may incur.<P>The more members a group has the lower they can make the costs at events because the budget will partially support the event.<P>2. You mention "some key officials from CFOG-I are from the Fiero Shop in California." - I only know of <I>one</I> official of this organization who is connected with Fiero Shop. That would be George Miller who is the owner of Fiero Shop.<P>One member is not the entire club and I would hope that this does not dissuade your decision to join us. <P>3. As for the installation advice you recieved from Fiero Shop concerning your 3.1 install, I cannot comment on that other than to say that my knowledge is that Fiero Shop does not do 3.1 installs and therefore probably wouldn't be your best source for that advice inasmuch as there are others more familiar with installing the 3.1.<P>Am I assuming that, by mentioning that there is not a Western Canada Regional Director that you are interested in that position?<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_TGO.jpg">     <P><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Chairman, Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A><BR><p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 12-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||2M4 Dale||12-12-2001||12:45 PM||||Yeah Voytek We want you as Canadian Western Regional Director........we're a great group !!! C'mon in !!!!!!!!!<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000014||fieroshop||12-12-2001||01:21 PM||||Votek<BR>If you had called me about your installation<BR>I would have been able to talk to you nad help as a 3.1, 2.8, and 3.4 are all in the same family class. With a few exceptions all are fairly easy to install.<BR>I don't recall you calling and asking for advice. Do you remember who you talked to?<P>------------------<BR> <BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieroshop_sig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Cheever3000||12-12-2001||01:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by webbee:<BR><B>Seems to me that we already have an international club that works really well,<BR>right here at Pennocks Fiero Forum.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>Honestly, I won't argue with you there.  If PFF provides everything you want, I'm happy for you.  Here's a quote from me on August 30, when we were just starting to gather some people together for the possibility of a new club: "...my reason for believing a strong relationship with PFF is so important is look at all the things that are good about it. It's quick, global <BR>communication, split into meaningful topics; it's archived; there are people there with excellent technical know-how; it has a pretty good listing of vendors and how to contact them; the club announcements <BR>section is something we could use; and it's moderated well. I can't even imagine attempting to come up with something better which would allow a new global club to interface with individual owners and <BR>clubs. It goes way beyond just having a website with a guestbook!"<P>I'm one of PFF's most ardent supporters.  One thing I want to accomplish which cannot be done here is reaching out in friendship to other types of car clubs and their enthusiasts.  Using a carefully planned strategy for improving the Fiero image and taking our rightful place of honor in the automotive world.  PFF is owned, run and moderated by one person.  He's doing a great job, don't misunderstand me, I'm just pointing out a difference.  CFOG-I is <B>not</B> and never will be run by a single person, and the people who do sit in the Office will be changed once in a while, by elections.<P>I do applaud all who get everything they need between PFF and their local club.  I wouldn't want to change what works for you.<BR>  <P><BR>||||reg||6
Z||000016||Voytek||12-12-2001||02:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by tgowens:<BR><B>Voytek,<P>Three things I would like to say to you here about your post........<P>1. I agree and understand about paying fees for an event only to have to turn around and pay another fee to join a club. There <I>is</I> a membership fee associated with the joining of CFOG-International. This membership fee is quite small when you realize that those funds will be used to provide the club magazine, merchandising which may be available, administrative costs such as mailing and postage fees, web site cost, telephone costs, etc ........ Any organization of this type will have to have a budget to work with, unless it is subsidized by a business, which this one is not. But, there <I>will</I> be reduced fees for its members at CFOG-International shows as opposed to the fees a non-member attendee may incur.<P>The more members a group has the lower they can make the costs at events because the budget will partially support the event.<P>2. You mention "some key officials from CFOG-I are from the Fiero Shop in California." - I only know of <I>one</I> official of this organization who is connected with Fiero Shop. That would be George Miller who is the owner of Fiero Shop.<P>One member is not the entire club and I would hope that this does not dissuade your decision to join us. <P>3. As for the installation advice you recieved from Fiero Shop concerning your 3.1 install, I cannot comment on that other than to say that my knowledge is that Fiero Shop does not do 3.1 installs and therefore probably wouldn't be your best source for that advice inasmuch as there are others more familiar with installing the 3.1.<P>Am I assuming that, by mentioning that there is not a Western Canada Regional Director that you are interested in that position?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>First off, Terry, I don't want to start a flame war here.  Those were just some of my concerns.<P>To address your comments, #1. I understand that costs are incurred if magazines, merchandise, etc. is made available and some of it (if not all) included in the members' fees.  No problem there.  My point was, there has to be an incentive to join and I was looking for someone to give me that incentive.  Having said that, I don't believe that the fees are too steep.<P>#2.  I don't recall saying that the entire Fiero Shop is involved in running the club.  BUT, is your name not Terry Owens?  The website shows someone by the name Terry Owens as the chairman.  Am I missing something here?  If I'm wrong, I apologize.  If you are, I expect no less.<P>#3.  I was aware that the Fiero Shop does not do 3.1 installs.  Does the green Fiero not belong to someone at the shop?  Does it not have a 3.1 in it?<P>You know, I really didn't want to get into this.  It happened in the spring when my 2.8 crapped out.  I emailed the Fiero Shop (since I believe the green Fiero has a 3.1) asking if, first, it is a possible and easy install, and, second, if I should watch out for anything in particular.  If the car DOESN'T have a 3.1 in it, I figured, if anyone might know the tricks of the trade, it would be the Fiero Shop.<P>Now, do you really want me to post the reply on here?  Again, to me it's water under the bridge - it was just a small concern w/ regards to joining a club whose key personnel belong to the same shop that provided no help to me.<P><B>To the Fiero Shop </B> (I don't know who posts under this name) - no, I did not phone in - I used e-mail.  Yes, I do know that you guys often post on PFF that advice is free and available.  You seem sincere enough and I'd like to believe that this is true.<P>Lastly, thanks for the invite to be the Western Canadian regional director.  Please let me know (PM if you wish) what the position involves - I'm interested in reading about it.<P>Thank you.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Alex4mula||12-12-2001||02:30 PM||||Very nice website. Hope enthusiasm drive this better than politics. If you really accomplish what you state I see no problem many people getting on it. FOCOA newsletter looks very good but I have never wanted to get the membership due to all the problems you stated. Really hope you do well.<P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red-Original Owner & White-1/94 with PM (:||||reg||1
Z||000018||tgowens||12-12-2001||02:37 PM||||Voytek,<P>First, yes my name <I>is</I> Terry Owens and I am indeed the Chairman. You made an assumption that I am Fiero Shop. I am not. I am at Fiero Shop restoring some of my own cars but am not responsible for the shop in any way.<P>Second, the green Fiero is George's car and is an '88GT with a stock 2.8L engine.||||reg||1
Z||000019||rogergarrison||12-12-2001||02:40 PM||||Im already in two very active car clubs. One with $10 dues, other with $20. $50 is pretty steep for a club membership unless it can do stuff for me. As a shop owner I already get at least a 25% discount on dealer parts. The few times I did call FOCOA, I couldnt even get a telephone answered. I think Ill just stay with what I got and freelance other events. <P>Dale, hope we can get together on the Vintage Gran Prix thing June 14-16. They also host the American LeMans Series on June 28-30. Everyone put them on your calendar. Camping is $20, sunday only=$22.00, weekend pass includes all areas; garage,infield,grandstands =$30.00<P>------------------<BR>           <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/rogergarrison1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||tgowens||12-12-2001||02:42 PM||||Roger - the $50 is for a CLUB to join - individual members dues are $25 .... ||||reg||1
Z||000021||fieroshop||12-12-2001||03:03 PM||||Her is an e-mail I just received answering The West Coast Fieros President Anthony Sisneros.<BR>I thought it would be appropriate so that everybody within the Fiero Community would know How and why CFOG-I was formed.<P>Anthony,<P>I recieved your email as I recieve all emails directed to CFOG-International.<P>Allow me to introduce myself, I am Terry Owens, Chairman of the Board of Classic Fiero Owners Group International. In addition I am a graphic artist and web designer and as such, designed the CFOG-I web site among<BR>many others that my company, West Highland Graphics & Design has created.<P>I would like to personally address a few of the points you made in your email........<P>CFOG-I is NOT trying to become the heir apparent of FOCOA. That was not the intention when we started and it never will be. We have stated from the beginning that we are an "alternative" and this was done because of<BR>the dissatisfaction of present and former members of FOCOA (of which I, myself am one). We felt that we could listen to the people and learn from the mistakes of other clubs such as FOCOA, FOA, etc and give a member a value for his money. This group is slightly more than two months old but it will continue to grow over time.<BR>Speaking from a web designer's point of view, there is NO excuse for a<BR>web site to be outdated. A webmaster's role is to assure the<BR>functionality of the web site but the content comes from the site owner.<P>There is also no excuse for allowing a domain name to expire, which was the case with FOCOA's domain name - that was an administrative error on the part of the site owners. I make sure that all of the sites that I create and host are up to date as to the domain name registration. But, what you are saying that, in over six months they have not been able to find a webmaster who could update the site......... I personally<BR>submitted a proposal to Gloria to do a make-over on their site and was promptly told that she had found someone who would do it for free. That is a large site and to perform maintenance and updates on that site free of charge is ludicrous. But, I accepted that -- evedintly is IS free as nothing at all has been done since it was posted back in August.<BR>Do not confuse certain issues here Anthony........ You made many a<BR>reference to San Leandro -- that was a show hosted by our local club before CFOG-International even began (CFOG-International began September 19 of this year) and as such there was no connection to this organization at all. Now, let's put the eggs in the proper basket --<BR>George was promoted to Western Regional Director by Joe Goodmon, not by Phil Huff. Joe did it to fill the position when he left to move to Chicago. George travelled all the way to Williamsburg to attend the Annual Board meeting only to be told that he could not because he was NOT the Western Regional Director because he had not been appointed by<BR>Phil. George Miller left the position because of the way he was treated, not to form CFOG-International. CFOG-International is MY creation as I had been working with several others who were not impressed with what<BR>our dues for FOCOA were providing us - in other words, I felt there should be something better and more a value for my money. In addition, George did not form CFOG (the local club) either - that, too, was my creation. We both left Golden Gate for our own reasons and I had already formed CFOG as a multi-state club when I was in Kentucky. It fit what we <BR>needed here and I transported it with me as I was the site and domain owner.<BR>The other fact is that, the web site that George showed you in San Leandro was ont that I was producing for FOCOA at Joe Goodmon's<BR>direction. It was presented to FOCOA at the meeting in Williamsburg and voted down. So, in essence, we (CFOG-International) are only doing what FOCOA said they would NOT do (I know this first hand too since I was at<BR>Williamsburg)<P>I applaud FOCOA for being very instrumental in helping Fieros West and San Diego put on a very Successful Western Regional in San Diego. They were NOT, however instrumental OR supportive of the show in San Leandro.<P>No, they don't hold the hand of every single club in the nation and to help where they can means that Phil and Phil alone will pick and choose who he helps and who is on his board.<BR>Personally, I respect Phil for his contribution to the Fiero community as all of us on the Board of Directors do. <BR>Personally I do not like Phil as I saw the bureaucratic approach FOCOA was taking toward their members in Louisville at the show two years ago.<P>Now, you said it all in this statement -- "FOCOA is a business" -- You are correct - there is FOCOA the business and FOCOA the club - the two should not be cunfused with each other nor should one subsidize the<BR>other. Personally, if I was paying dues to an organization, their finances ARE my business, I am a part owner! With any Corporation there<BR>are shareholders and stockholders - they are furnished with an accounting of where the money goes....... I for one am not going to just send $34 to somebody to get two of the four magazines that I am promised, to have to fight to get a membership card, to be treated like an idiot at one of their national events........ -- no, I want more<BR>value for my dollar just as those who began CFOG-International want............ and, they WILL get it. I am not just a business owner,<BR>I am a multiple Fiero Owner, I am on the Board of Directors of two other <BR>corporations and have been in the business worlkd for over thirty years dealing with the public. What I can tell you is this, customer<BR>satisfaction speaks volumes about an organization -- that's why CFOG-International was started.<P>While it may appear that some are basing the worth of FOCOA on their national event (that's all they have) I would just like to assure you that, no matter what you thought of San Leandro, it was NOT a function<BR>of CFOG-International. In fact, the show site in San Leandro was much more suitable for a show than the site in Williamsburg in a school parking lot with school busses for a background and a locked building<BR>with no restroom facilities. Basically, San Leandro was a show put on by a very small (at that time) local club with NO support of FOCOA. -- Frankly, I think it is an unfair comparison.<P>Also, what George was "pitching" in San Leandro was the FOCOA package that Joe Goodmon was trying to drive through but was shot down by FOCOA in Williamsburg, that's why you never heard anything more about it -<BR>Phil killed it in Williamsburg. As I said, CFOG-International was not even formed at that point.<P>"If it is sad what the potential of FOCOA was, what have you done to help it? If you contacted Phil and offered assistance in some way than you could have been a part of the solution and not one of the masses<BR>that points out the problems with no solution of their own." -- I, as many others on our Board DID try to help FOCOA - we paid<BR>annual dues. I, personally was very involved with Joe Goodmon to try to bring FOCOA into a progressive organization and I have all the<BR>documentation between Joe and myself to support my claim. The problem was, Joe was working behind Phil's back and when it all came to a head in Williamsburg, Joe sat there and did not open his mouth about all of<BR>this. Personally, I felt somewhat disgusted since I had spent quite a bit of time and resources on preparing a web site since that was what Joe wanted. In the end, it was evident that Phil is running the organization and it will always be that way - no, it is NOT member run -- it is difficult for me to support an organization by sending them<BR>money when I realize that it is a 13 year old organization that does not even have a Treasurer to account for the funds. -- At any rate - there have been MANY people who have tried to be a part of the solution but as long as Phil will not relinquish any control to an organized body of Officers and Directors, it will never happen.<P>Now, respecting your position as President of Fieros West and your<BR>position with FOCOA, I am asking you to respect MY position as Chairman<BR>of CFOG-International. No one is accusing Phil of not working hard. But,<P>I'll tell you one BIG difference in this organization and what you stated -- this does NOT put food on my table nor pay my mortgage - that is NOT the function of a club - a business maybe but not a club -- a club is to be established as a non-profit corporation and as such, should not be putting food on anyone's table nor paying their mortgage.<BR>That is sheer exploitation and is against the California Non-Profit Corporation laws.<P>Now, as you stated, you "percieved" the implication to be something other than what it is. Perception is a funny thing - we can percieve ourselves one way while someone else percieves us totally differently. I <BR>welcome your letter and I hope that you welcome this response as I feel that there have been several things which were leaving you with a false impression of CFOG-International.<P>If we succeed it is only because we take the time to provide factual, informative and up-to-date information to the Fiero Owners. We are and intend to do just that. But, not with an outdated web site and mis-information printed right on the cover page for all to see.<P>Now, If I appeared as rude, I also apologize -- I am not into bashing anyone or any thing but, it helps to review and determine the lagitimacy and plausibility of an organization if the true facts are known. This is what I have tried to portray here.<P>I appreciate your response as I recognize that it took your time and much thought went into it. It is because of responses such as yours that CFOG-International will continue to grow and be sucessful..<P>In advance, whishing you and yours a very happy and joyous holiday season.<P>Terry Owens, Chairman<BR>CFOG-International<P><BR><P>------------------<BR> <BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieroshop_sig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000022||Formula88||12-12-2001||03:13 PM||||So if the $50 Club membership gets an individual a discount on membership, what's the discount?  What are the incentives for new member renewals?<P>Also, since CFOG-I is sponsored by the Fiero Shop (dicounted parts for members), and with both George Miller and Terry Owens the top two ranking individuals in the club, that strengthens the Fiero Shop tie.  I'm not sure that's a good thing.  How about some sponsorship or discounts for other vendors?  The Fiero Store, Rodney Dickman, V8 Archie, Mr. Mikes?  I'd hate to think the only reason The Fiero Shop is offering discounts is that the President of the club has a vested interest in it's business.  <P>You've got some great ideas.  Forgive my skepticism, but to be honest, I've seen too many flame wars involving both Terry and George to be too enthusiastic.  I will watch cautiously to see how things develop.  I geniunely hope things go well, and if I see positive growth and results, I'll consider membership.  I especially like the group insurance rates and POCI affiliation - grand ideas!<P>Perhaps NC Fieros will consider a Club membership so we can get discounted memberships.  That would give us the benefit of discounted shows, etc.  Not a bad incentive, there.<P>Good luck, guys.  ||||reg||14
Z||000023||fieroshop||12-12-2001||03:23 PM||||Fomula88,<P>All the businesses have the ability to join CFOG-I and give member discounts.<BR>I have paid CFOG-I my $25.00 vender membership which allows me to also to advertize in the magazine. Cost to advertizs is $100.00 for a quarter page ad to be run in each issue for one year. (4 issues)<P>All Fiero Venders can do the same. <BR>They will also be listed as sponsor of CFOG-I||||reg||1
Z||000024||tgowens||12-12-2001||03:40 PM||||Formula88,<P>$50 for a club to join - $25 for an individual to join -- clubs will have benefits for their dues which are different than the benefits for an individual. The purpose here is to encourage an individual to join both the International organization as well as either joining a local club or forming one.<P>Now, I have stated this over and over and over again - even right above this post - I AM the Chairman of CFOG-International. I am NOT Fiero Shop. I have absolutely NO legal nor financial responsibility for Fiero Shop and I do NOT make decisions for Fiero Shop aned I do NOT operate Fiero Shop.<P>Now, if that is a bad thing, as you have suggested, then I cannot be responsible for you feeling that way as I am not sure what you feel is a "bad thing".<P>Fiero Shop is a sponsor inasmuch as they have donated time and resources to CFOG-International. In fact, other business wishing to or willing to do so would be considered a sponsor as well. This is different than paying a fee as a business member for the purposes of advertising your company on the web site and in the magazine.<P>Any of those you mentioned, Rodney, Mr. Mike, Archie, etc are free to join us if they desire. <P>Now, there are those out there who will let the actions of one man or one business keep them from joining an organization comprised of MANY members - not just that man or business. That is a sad state of affairs to me -- I want to have fun with my cars and I want to belong to an organization which will further my knowledge, education and enjoyment of my cars -- but - I want to belong to one which will provide a value for my dollar -- slightly over $2 a month is a small amount to join an organization which has and will have world wide exposure -- I have conversed with Fiero owners from South America, Europe, Canada, Australlia and the U.S. because of what CFOG-International is doing -- events are being planned in many locations and I am going to attend as many as possible and have fun and I am not going to let one man or one business stop me from having my fun (just MY opinion).||||reg||1
Z||000025||Formula88||12-12-2001||03:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LZeitgeist:<BR><B>...I don't know how much input Curry Obenshain had ...<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> <IMG SRC="http://www.data-techniques.net/cwm/ups/DeNiro/big_eyes.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000026||Voytek||12-12-2001||03:44 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by tgowens:<BR><B>Voytek,<P>First, yes my name <I>is</I> Terry Owens and I am indeed the Chairman. You made an assumption that I am Fiero Shop. I am not. I am at Fiero Shop restoring some of my own cars but am not responsible for the shop in any way.<P>Second, the green Fiero is George's car and is an '88GT with a stock 2.8L engine.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Like I said - apologies if I'm wrong.  I am still a bit puzzled, though.  Whenever someone posts something about the Fiero Shop (complaint or otherwise) you are the first one to comment/defend the shop.  Kinda strange, considering that you have nothing to do with the shop, don't ya think?  Now you know why I made the assumption.<P>Now, since you are the one engaging yourself in flame wars on behalf of the Fiero Shop, you definitely didn't give me the best impression of the Shop and made me have second thoughts about purchasing anything from them.  I am planning on buying exhaust, suspension and a few other goodies in the coming year.  Now that I know you're not related, I will consider the Fiero Shop again - thanks for clarifying that.<P>Also, (like I said earlier) - if the green Fiero has a 2.8 in it, that's fine.  For some reason I thought there was a green Fiero (dragon or something) related to someone at the shop with a 3.1 in it.  Whether true or not (I really don't care anymore) I thought - AS I ALREADY STATED - that, if anyone could give this type of advice, it would be the Fiero Shop.<P>Done and done.<P>I don't wish to touch on this subject again.  The Fiero community is a relatively small one, with only a handful of vendors.  I wish all vendors well (despite some of the wars) as it is ultimately to my benefit.  I also believe that the CFOG-I is a great idea with good potential.<P>I will leave this alone with a final suggestion: the reputation of the Fiero vendors is, in part, generated here on PFF.  Please consider that, Terry.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000027||Formula88||12-12-2001||03:51 PM||||Terry, I never said you were the Fiero Shop.  George; however, is.  I simply implied you have ties to the Fiero Shop, even if you're aren't directly employed by them.  If that was vague or incorrect, I stand corrected.  <P>And you and George have repeatedly been of like mind in many arguments and flame wars here.  Would I let concerns about 1 person keep me from belonging to an organization?  Well, that all depends.  Is the person just a member, or President of the club?  See, this is where previous demeanor has the potential to damage a person's credibility, or by association, the credibility of an organization which that person is in charge of.  <P>Your website says you can get "Discounted membership for individuals who are a part of a member club."  Does that mean my individual membership is less than $25 if I belong to a member club?  If not, what's the discount?<P>I'm really not trying to badger you.  I have my concerns and I'll act accordingly.  I agree in spirit with everthing CFOG-I wants to accomplish.  If I see that's actually happening, I'll be glad to poney up the membership fee.  I wish you the best of luck.  <p>[This message has been edited by Formula88 (edited 12-12-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000028||tgowens||12-12-2001||04:16 PM||||Voytek, I have the impression from your post that your argument is with me rather than with George OR Fiero Shop - if so, please email me directly at  <A HREF="mailto:terry@tgowens.com">terry@tgowens.com</A>  so that we can clear this up.<P>Formula88, the dues <I>were</I>$40 for an individual <I>not</I> belonging to a CFOG-I afilliated club, however, at the present time (per the decision of the Board of Directors) the membership fee for an individual is $25 per year.||||reg||1
Z||000029||Formula88||12-12-2001||04:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by tgowens:<BR><B>Formula88, the dues <I>were</I>$40 for an individual <I>not</I> belonging to a CFOG-I afilliated club, however, at the present time (per the decision of the Board of Directors) the membership fee for an individual is $25 per year.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks for clearing that up.  So that's $25 whether or not I'm a member of a member club.  So, I don't get any discount for belonging to a member club.  That's all I wanted to know.<P>Thanks for your time and attention to detail!||||reg||14
Z||000030||tgowens||12-12-2001||04:45 PM||||Formula88, while I responded to your post and am the Chairman of CFOG-International I must say that the decision to lower the membership fee to $25 was a Board decision and not necessarily mine. This group is new and growing but it was felt that it was necessary to temporarily lower the membership dues in order to be an incentive to join. <P>I apologize for any confusion that this may have caused you and sincerely hope that you will make a choice to join CFOG-International. As the organization grows there will be many member benefits to come along.<P>Also, to those who feel that I engage in flame wars, whether on behalf of Fiero Shop or otherwise let me just say this. I feel very strongly about character and integrity and those who know me will attest to the fact that if I say I'll do something I do it. Flame wars? No, but, I will defend character and integrity and post facts as I have them or correct inaccuracies as I know them. That is not a flame war - I do not and will not engage in the bashing of anyone or any thing - everyone has the right to their opinion and I have the right to mine as well, but, when I voice mine it is not considered a "flame war" any more than when you voice <I>your</I> opinion.<P>I am looking forward to the growth and success of CFOG-International and am proud to be associated with the individuals who have joined its roll so far. There are a lot of wonderful people on board and a wealth of knowledge within them.<P>What I want is what all other Fiero owners want - to be able to maintain and enjoy our Fieros and have fun doing it. That is the basic premise behind CFOG-International.<p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 12-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000031||MinnGreenGT||12-12-2001||05:00 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Voytek:<BR><B> Also, (like I said earlier) - if the green Fiero has a 2.8 in it, that's fine.  For some reason I thought there was a green Fiero (dragon or something)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Voytek- Actually I believe that you are correct... the Green "Dragon" (PFF Member: Dragon) does have the 3.1L in question. As does my Green Car (yup... they're all nearly the same green too).  But Dragon is in Florida as far as I know.<BR>------------------------------------------<P>Anyway- for those that haven't checked the site to see who the board members of CFOG-i really are... it's worth a look.  I am the Deputy Director of the North Central Region.  You may also notice that there is more than one Bartlett listed   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Don't worry, we're not related.  I'm not trying to drop names here (OK... AUSFiero, Songman, FieroWife, 87ECO5, and more!)  This should simply demonstrate that we (CFOG-i) are an actual organization- not a store running a club or a club running a store.  Instead we are a diverse group of Fiero enthusiasts who are dedicating our spare time (although sparse as it is) to the goal of creating and expanding an international non-profit Fiero oriented club!<P>As an organization we (the board) voted to lower all personal-level memberships to $25 (at least for an introductory period). To try to appeal to everyone- irregardless of your contact with a local-level Fiero organization.<P>What I'm really trying to show here, is that this is quite a difference from the FOCOA that you're used to.  I had some experience with the FOCOA back about 5-7 years ago- since then I've all but forgotten/ignored them.  Especially considering how they treated my Dad when he stopped by their "Fiero Museum" while visiting California.  One of the primary differences we are going for here is contact, communication, and information for our members.  We clearly list our emails & phone numbers for easy reference on the personell page of the website. We offer a Toll-Free support line for that time when you're in the middle of a project and don't know where to turn (yes, the forum is great & usually fast- but faster than a free phone call?) just grab the phone and dial!<P>As with any startup company or organization... there is a growth period where everything may not run as smoothly as planned (trust me, I just started my <A HREF="http://www.romatekgraphics.com" TARGET=_blank>own company</A>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">).  But have a little faith.<P>------------------<BR>               <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by MinnGreenGT (edited 12-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000032||tgowens||12-12-2001||05:08 PM||||Rob, you are correct about the green Fiero with the 3.1 - that belongs to one of our members, Frank Martin in Florida.......<P>Voytek, Frank can be emailed through his club which is listed on our web site in the Club section in the Southeast Region||||reg||1
Z||000033||Voytek||12-12-2001||05:16 PM||||Terry - I don't have an argument.<P>You've got mail.<P>I just read your reply above.  For cryin' out loud TERRY!  I already told you, I had the car confused with you guys!  It doesn't change the fact that I did e-mail the Fiero Shop.  No, I do not want to talk to the owner anymore - that was 6 friggin months ago.<P>Can I make this any clearer?<p>[This message has been edited by Voytek (edited 12-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000034||Voytek||12-12-2001||05:28 PM||||Thanks for the clarification, MinnGreen.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <BR>The club sounds like a great idea, indeed.||||reg||1
Z||000035||72vinman||12-12-2001||05:31 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MinnGreenGT:<BR>[B] Instead we are a diverse group of Fiero enthusiasts who are dedicating our spare time (although sparse as it is) to the goal of creating and expanding an international non-profit Fiero oriented club!<BR>B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I have to agree with MinnGreenGT. As Regional Director for Central Canada, I have never met my fellow directors in person (yet). However, through countless emails and online director meetings I have been able to assess the fact that we are all in this for the same thing. We all love Fiero's and want to enjoy them as well as meet other people that want to do the same. And it all starts with two-way communication, which is what this organization has been built on. I welcome any suggestions or comments any Fiero owners from Alberta, Manitoba and Ontario have. On the CFOG-I personnel page my phone number is also listed if you wish to contact me.<P>I look forward to talking with many of you soon.<BR>Best Wishes for a Happy Holiday<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigvin2_1.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by 72vinman (edited 12-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000036||LZeitgeist||12-12-2001||06:25 PM||||Wow... politics. Who'da thunk?<P>Life is too short to get involved in more stuff like this - especially with a price tag. <P>I'll pass - thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000037||Formula88||12-12-2001||11:33 PM||||I guess I'm just gunshy after experiences with other organizations (not just FOCOA).<P>I wish you the best of luck - truly.  ||||reg||14
