Z||000042||tgowens||12-13-2001||01:25 AM||||Formula88, I understand "gunshy" -- I'll tell you what -- fill out a membership application on our membership page and I will pay for your membership for the first year - then if, after the first year, you decide it isn't what you like, just don't renew. If it is, however, I would like for you to tell others about what we are doing, and then renew ....... fair enough?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_TGO.jpg">  <P><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
Z||000043||CoolBlue87GT||12-13-2001||09:53 AM||||Speaking of the Williamsburg show, incase anyone wants to see pic's of the show, here's an address or two<P>(please let me know if these paths do not work)<BR> <A HREF="http://www.ofoto.com/I.jsp?m=17619718203&n=180995269" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ofoto.com/I.jsp?m=17619718203&n=180995269</A> <P> <BR>(this 2nd path, has 12 pages, displayed one at a time)<BR><A HREF="http://communities.msn.com/SuncoastFieroClub/williamsburg2001fieroshow.msnw" TARGET=_blank>http://communities.msn.com/SuncoastFieroClub/williamsburg2001fieroshow.msnw</A> <P>Hope you enjoy.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/CoolBluesig.gif">     <P><p>[This message has been edited by CoolBlue87GT (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000044||ChopTop||12-13-2001||11:24 AM||||My comments are not meant to discourage a new international/national organization, but may offer insight as to why car clubs may have a more difficult time attracting membership since the advent of the Internet.  <BR>After reading this thread so far the question I’m asking myself is; why should I join a Fiero international organization?  First a little background info.  I purchased my 85GT back in the fall of 1988 (still have it along with two others), and shortly afterward joined Fiero Owners Club of America (FOCOA).  I purchased many parts from them, and attended a few of the national shows when they were held in my area.  By the mid 90s I was tiring of the infrequent newsletters along with a few other concerns, and decided it was no longer in my interest to remain a member.  I then joined my state club, Michigan Fiero Club.  While I do not attend monthly meetings because of the distance, I do receive the club newsletter (on time), and can participate in club activities if I choose too.  <BR>I then discovered the Pennock’s Fiero Forum (PFF) about a year ago and found a place that I can access anytime of the day or night.  I can sell parts, buy parts, receive troubleshooting help, help others when I can, make contact with other members in my area, receive encouragement and support, all just a few clicks away.  Basically PFF has become the Fiero cyber club with a few exceptions, no officers to elect, no dues to pay, and no meetings to attend.  No one man or board deciding on what we are going to do.  The only thing that is asked by the administrator of this site is that we remain civil to each other.  That’s a pretty small price to pay in exchange for a site that offers it’s membership a wealth of information and the ability to pretty much ask anything that’s on their mind.  And while PFF does not offer a national show, there is nothing stopping the members from organizing an event.<BR>What would it take for me to join a new national/international Fiero club?  I’m not sure, I’m not opposed to paying membership dues as long as I feel I am receiving a good value for my money.  But again I have to ask myself, can they supply me with something I am not already receiving from PFF or my state club?  The only benefits that come to mind are receiving a monthly newsletter, and the possibility of attending a national show…if one is ever held, and if it’s within a reasonable distance for me.  In spite of my feelings, I do believe the Fiero would benefit from having a national organization that would promote consistency in judging, be democratically run, and promote the positive attributes of the Fiero.  <BR>Good luck on your continued efforts.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000045||tgowens||12-13-2001||12:41 PM||||ChopTop,<P>You said in your post, <I>"I do believe the Fiero would benefit from having a national organization that would promote consistency in judging, be democratically run, and promote the positive attributes of the Fiero."</I> -- These are a few of the reasons CFOG-International was created. In addition, we realized that having just one club meeting per year was not conducive to good operations of a club and we hold our Board meetings online once a month to assure smooth operations of the organization. We also felt that holding one national show per year was, for the most part, eliminating much of the Fiero community due to the distance they had to travel to get to the show so we plan to hold two national shows per year, one in the western half of the country and one in the eastern half of the country. At least this will allow the Fiero community more of an opportunity to attend a national show. We might even have a vote, after the shows, in some manner to determine a "Grand Champion" of sorts -- point being, we want to provide things that haven't been provided before, eliminate some of the problems associated with past and previous clubs of this type as well as make this truly an "International" club rather than just a "National" one.<P>I realize the value of Pennock's Forum as well as it is a valuable tool and source of entertainment for us all. We just felt that the idea of a "club" for the Fiero community, as we know it, should be expanded upon and enhanced, in order to provide its membership a greater value for their money - this is what we are attempting to do. <P>This organization is not the answer to everyone's needs and some may be better served with just their local club. We are attempting to put together an organization comprised of, not only the local clubs but the individual members out there who are in areas that do not even have a local club. No, this organization will never achieve the utopian status of having all Fiero owners and enthusiasts on its roll but to the ones that do join we promise a value for their dollar and a lot of fun along the way.<P>We are stressing concepts that should (but not always are) fundamental to any organization - that we are accessible and accountable to our membership, that we deliver what we promise and deliver it on time, and that we respond to members in a proper and timely fashion with a resolution to their needs and requests.<P>Now, you mentioned, <I>"I’m not opposed to paying membership dues as long as I feel I am receiving a good value for my money."</I> -- that's exactly what all of us were saying too when we created CFOG-International. All in all it's really not a bad deal for $25 a year......<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_TGO.jpg">   <P><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A><p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000046||Formula88||12-13-2001||01:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by tgowens:<BR><B>Formula88, I understand "gunshy" -- I'll tell you what -- fill out a membership application on our membership page and I will pay for your membership for the first year - then if, after the first year, you decide it isn't what you like, just don't renew. If it is, however, I would like for you to tell others about what we are doing, and then renew ....... fair enough?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That is a most generous offer.  I feel I must decline, though.  Why should I get a membership for free?  What about other people sitting on the fence?  Are you going to give them free memberships too?<P>I genuinely appreciate the offer.  I just don't think it's a good idea.  Thank you just the same.||||reg||14
Z||000047||Cheever3000||12-13-2001||02:45 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>What about other people sitting on the fence?  Are you going to give them free memberships too?<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>As a matter of fact, at least three of us in the organization have made that offer.  The reason is to induce folks to try it.  It's one years' dues, not a lifetime membership, but if we bring in some who observe and then describe to others what they see, we believe it would be a good thing.  We have nothing to hide, and are willing to let some join in risk-free.  I made the same offer in an O/T topic on October 25.  Nobody took me up on it yet, so it still stands for the first one to ask.  Understand, though, that we're still getting started, so the benefits won't be earth-shattering, but you will see our sincerity, and your input can help steer our direction.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000048||BOILERMAKER||12-13-2001||03:52 PM||||I offered a partial memebership payment on my behalf the last time the locals got together with me and only one person took me up on it.||||reg||1
Z||000049||Formula88||12-13-2001||11:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cheever3000:<BR><B> Understand, though, that we're still getting started, so the benefits won't be earth-shattering, but you will see our sincerity, and your input can help steer our direction.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'm seeing some of that sincerity here.  And if I continue to see the sincerity, I'll gladly purchase a membership.  I don't mean any slight in declining the free offer.  If I'm going to be a memeber, I'd rather purchase the membership.<P>Thanks again. ||||reg||14
Z||000050||tgowens||12-14-2001||01:13 AM||||Formula88, I respect your position and I applaud you for feeling that way (besides, it just saved me twenty five bucks!!) (j/k). I personally look forward to seeing you join with us at some point in time. In the meantime - you and yours have a happy holiday season......<P>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000051||qwikgta||12-14-2001||11:08 PM||||As a member of the Host club (Fiero Owners Club of Southeast Virginia) I want to clear the air.  We had a deal with a local vendor who was going to provide 3-4 "Port a Johns" at the school the morning of the event.  The individual never showed, we had an deal, he had other plans.  As for the class change, it was news to us too, I was in the 88, t-top, coupe with 60-100K miles class, and I was the only one there, I was sure I was going to get my first trophy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">, I still have never been given an answer for why it happened.  For those who have never put on a "National Show" it is not that easy.  Now if we were a Corvette club or something flashey we would have gotten a local radio station to spend the day with us but when we attempted to generate business as "A Fiero Club" most people said, "Theres a club for Fiero's".  I wish the folks next year the best of luck, and I for one will not complain if things are not perfect.  <P>No flames intended, just passing information||||reg||8
Z||000052||tgowens||12-15-2001||12:15 AM||||qwikgta, I can appreciate things like that as I have been involved with staging everything from "oldies" shows to car shows to a classical music festival -- things do not always go right. That's why you need the "what if" plan...... what if this doesn't work we'll do that........<P>And, my opinion is that the "host club" should not have had to do the work of setting up FOCOA's national show - they could have had an "assisting" role but you guys did everything from arrainging (or trying to) toilets to posting the information and reservation forms on YOUR web site!<P>As for class changes -- unacceptable in my book -- classes should not be complicated but, there are always those who can take something simple and make them complicated. I believe that the original idea is to have lots of classes so that more people have the opportunity to win a trophy but when not enough cars show up the classes change -- that's not quite right to me........ a lot of people make the concious decision to show their car depending on what class they fall into and once registered in that class it should remain that way --<P>Here's something else that wasn't right - I remember when I drove all the way from Paso Robles, CA to Louisville to attend the FOCOA show in 2000. I actually drove 263 miles further (according to MapQuest) than Joe Goodmon who drove from San Diego but he was presented the trophy for longest distance travelled. He was on FOCOA's Board, I was not, need I say more?<P>You can count on this - CFOG-International WILL be operated differently ...<P>just MY 3 cents...........<p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 12-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000053||Formula88||12-15-2001||12:32 AM||||Well, the porta-john situation was a bummer, and an unfortunate one.  When I was about to ask Curry about rest room facilities, I heard him talking to someone else about it already.  He said, "That was Phil's job.  I guess he doesn't kick as much a$$ as I do."  That pretty much let me know what kind of help to expect.  When I finally did confront Curry about it and made my displeasure known, he got very defensive and told me that Phil had taken care of it by paying the gas station on the corner so they'd let us use their facilities.  This was never announced, that I heard.  So no one really knew that Phil was trying to take care of the situation.<P>Phil seems to try very hard.  But running a national club is too big of a job for 1 man.  He doesn't seem to understand the concept of delegation.  I'm not even going to discuss my opinoin of Curry.<P>The classes for the show, at least the stock classes broke down to:<BR>Unrestored - &lt;50% resotred - &gt;50% restored - 100% restored<BR>Coupe - Formula - GT<BR>Hardtop - Sunroof - T-Top<BR>&lt;60K miles - 60K-120K miles - &gt;120K miles<BR>[note: this is from memory, but that pretty well sums it up]<P>You picked one from each selection.  That put me in the:<P>Stock,  &lt;50% restored, Formula, Sunroof, 60-12K miles.<P>LZ's car was in Stock, Unrestored, Formula, Sunroof, &lt;60K miles.  This was great, because we wouldn't be competing against one another.<P>When the judging was done, that was condensed to: Stock Formula.  Now, my "project" car is competing against fully restored and low-mileage original cars.  I'm not upset that I didn't win a trophy.  But if I'd known that's how the classes were gonna be done, I'd have saved the $50 bucks and just gone to the show to watch.  That would have even given me some time for sight seeing in beautiful Williamsburg - rather than spending Thurs. and Fri. detailing my car.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000054||2M4 Dale||12-15-2001||12:49 AM||||I have a serious question regarding the leadership of FOCOA.......Here it is...Why don't the leadership of FOCOA participate in the greatest, largest, most used Fiero Forum on the planet ??? Seems to me that if FOCOA wanted to keep it's membership happy and growing they would visit this forum and post topics and updates ... etc. <P>I am / was a member of FOCOA and I would sure understand  FOCOAs not keeping thier website updated if they were using this "FREE" (THANKS CLIFF) forum as a chance to air club topics. <P>Would any FOCOA leadership personel care to respond to this ??? I dought it !<P>One other thing, that's on the table for us (CFOG-I), is the thought of somehow establishing a relationship, of some sort, to POCI...(Pontiac Oakland Club International). I really don't get it that FOCOA hasn't thought of this. Or if they have why they havn't already got something going with POCI. Fieros are Pontiacs !!! Fiero owners are welcomed by POCI with open arms !!! And the Fiero prescents at POCI meets is growing rapidly. <P>quikgta you said and I quote "Now if we were a Corvette club or something flashey we would have gotten a local radio station to spend the day with us but when we attempted to generate business as "A Fiero Club" most people said, "Theres a club for Fiero's".<P>Why hasn't the whole FOCOA experiance better proven to the automotive enthusiest that we do , indeed , have a very worthwile little sports car here. Afterall the Pontiac Fiero is a very, very unique automobile !!!<P>It is my intent as a officer of this club, CFOG-I, to see to it that my Fiero and YOUR Fiero garners the respect it deserves in the automotive collector/perfomance car world !!!<P>Ok I'm done ranting......lol||||reg||1
Z||000055||Pondski||12-15-2001||01:08 AM||||I miss the Bay Area! A drive up there might be in order. Richmond hasn't changed in 20 years -- has it? <P>------------------<BR>Thanks, Chris <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/wbsmileyus.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000056||tgowens||12-15-2001||01:27 AM||||Chris, I haven't been here for 20 years but just lock your doors before you drive in here...........<P>Formula88, I personally cannot see the need for the greater than and less than 50% brackets - that's what I was talking about when I said someone could take something simple and make it complicated - it's either restored or it isn't, right? what if I think mine is 51% restored and you think yours is 49% restored? (it really doesn't matter anyway 'cause I don't have a Formula, all of mine are GT's    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)<P>Our show classes won't be complicated at all, I can assure you of that. They will be fair and fairly judged. Here's the catch though, you pre-register your car and you really don't know who you are competing against - but - you will be in the proper class and NOT changed.<P>As for the mention of porta-pottys, we have basically decided that our shows will be at self-contained locations - in other words, the hotel will be there, the show /field/lot will be there, everything needed will be on premises.<P>Now, the radio station thing - the way I do it is to prepare a press kit which is released to local newspapers and radio stations prior to the event. I contact a local TV station and arrange for them to provide a live-feed the day of the show --<P>All the i's are dotted and the t's crossed by the organization with the local host club assisting - not the other way around which is what sounds like happened there. If WE are staging a National show why should it be YOUR job to do the work??<P>Sheesh - I'm ranting just like Dale........<P>By the way Dale, I am still negotiating with POCI and a couple of other groups as well to align ourselves with them......... and, by the way, Pennock's Forum is not recognized by FOCOA as being something that they want to participate in (those were the words that <I>I</I> heard!)<p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 12-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000057||LZeitgeist||12-15-2001||08:35 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>As for the mention of porta-pottys, we have basically decided that our shows will be at self-contained locations - in other words, the hotel will be there, the show /field/lot will be there, everything needed will be on premises.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have been informed by Curry that they had made arrangements with the Ramada Inn (where the FOCOSEVA gathering was the previous year) to hold the show in the parking lot again, but the hotel changed their mind at the last minute. That necessitated finding a new, easy-to-find location on short notice. <P>I have also been informed by Curry that arrangements had been made to have toilets brought to the site, but that the company providing them failed to show. So instead, arrangements were made with the gas station on the corner to allow us to use their restroom. <P>Evidently nobody running the show saw the huge "OUT OF ORDER" sign that the gas station posted on the restroom door about an hour after the show started. Evidently the arrangements made weren't satisfactory with the gas station after all.<P>Another thing Curry asked me was whether or not I was aware that "at the Fiero Festivals in 1990 and 1994, at the Indy Motor Speedway, or a later year at Nashville, there were no bathroom facilities within 1/4 mile? Nobody complained about that."<P>In regard to that, I have to say that I can't *imagine* nobody complaining about that situation - but even beyond that, I would think if the Health Department had heard about a pre-planned large public gathering of this sort with no restroom facilities available *anywhere* even close to the site, they'd have come in and either fined the organization running the show, revoked whatever permits (if any) were necessary, or just shut the show down. <P>If Curry wants anything more said about the Williamsburg situation, he will have to post here himself, or get qwikgta to continue speaking for him. ||||reg||1
Z||000058||LZeitgeist||12-15-2001||08:40 AM||||Oh, for clarification, no, was not at any of the gatherings at the Indy Motor Speedway or Nashville, so I am not personally aware of what went on there. People who were there can portray a more accurate picture of events there.||||reg||1
Z||000059||qwikgta||12-15-2001||10:11 AM||||Hey LZ,  I am not speaking for Curry, I happened upon this thread and just wanted to clear up some confusion about the toliets.  The reason that the "out of order" sign was put up was that the gas station was not happy with dozens of people using their facilities when the intended use of their facilities is for the patrons of the gas station, not a bunch of people who are at a car show next door.  They put up the sign to keep people from using up their stock of paper and all the water that was being flushed, with out some king of $$$$ to offset it.  That is why Phil gave them the $$$ so that they would open the doors again. And they did. <P>The reason that we could not have the show at the hotel is that we did not have half of the hotel sold out for the show.  The hotel was soldout but we did not have 50% of it.  Because we didn't get the expected turnout we could not use the parking lot at the hotel.  We had planned on around 200 cars and only 105 showed.   <P>Again, I am not speaking for Curry, I spend more time here than he does.  Curry spent a great deal of time working on this with Phil.  We as a club have gotten more crap about how much sucked than what was good, it starts to piss you off after a while.<P>Rob||||reg||1
Z||000060||WKDFIRO||12-15-2001||11:16 AM||||POCI is NOT as receptive in some areas to Fieros as in others. It took years to patch up things with our local POCI when they unceramoniously threw Fieros West out of their car show many years ago. As I began to get to know the current President of POCI, Greg Miller (who was not President at the time) we were able untangle the misunderstanding. But I still couldn't get some of our key members to even deal with POCI in our area. Since then we have enjoyed car shows with POCI of San Diego with their beautiful vineyard car show (I think it is Dearpark, I can't remember right now) and an offshoot of POCI, the Outlaw Pontiac Racers Association. <P>The times I have been able to get things rolling, we have run into certain members (of POCI, Outlaw Pontiacs have been the only ones to truely welcome us) who simply scoff the Fieros, (usually younger members) and create a feeling of hostility when they tell us to "get a real car." I know that this is isolated, I know that they don't represent the whole of the organization, but it sure doesn't keep our members coming back. <P>Its just a shame right now, because of low participation that we can't take advantage of some of these opportunities to mend fences. ||||reg||9
Z||000061||WKDFIRO||12-15-2001||11:22 AM||||It would also seem that the Terry Owens/Fiero Shop misunderstanding stemmed from George reprinting an e-mail from Terry to me under his (Fiero Shop) name. <P>Gotta be careful of the funny perception thing...||||reg||2
Z||000062||FIERO JOHN-WI||12-15-2001||12:14 PM||||I have been watching this thread since it started and I just want to tell you my take.<BR> (1)I look at it like this, if you are STRICKLY into SHOWING your Fiero for REAL TUFF COMPETITION ONLY then POCI is the one for you (no politics as far as favorites and official judge classes or peoples choice classes) <BR> (2)If you are strickly into having a "GREAT TIME', BIG FIERO CRUISE ALL TOGETHER, FIERO CAR MODEL CONTEST, FIERO BAR "BONDING" and FIERO CAR SHOW COMPETITION is SECONDAIRY then the MIDWEST FIERO REGIONAL is the one for you (11TH ANNUAL THIS YEAR)<BR> (3)If you are strickly into DRIVING your Fiero EVERYDAY no matter the day or forecast and want to be commended by other fiero owners like yourself and be REALLY APPRECIATED by all FIERO OWNERS for that day, then FIERO-RAMA is the one for you.<BR> (4)If you are strickly into GIANT and I mean Giant FIERO and other cars as well CAR show with a Giant swap meet all together in one area, plus fantastic weather and can't forget a great time with pictures on the beach, then the FIERO DAYTONA RUN in Florida is the one for you.<BR> (5)If you are strickly into MEETING "OLD FIERO FRIENDS AND MEETING NEW ONES" with competition being LAST on your list then FOCOA is the one for you.<P>And I think if you look at it like this and if you can look at YOURSELF and say this is where I fit in  BEST, then thats where you should go! <P>REMEMBER NOW! There are MANY other Fiero EVENTS that I DIDN'T MENTION and I HOPE no other FIERO CLUB OR FIERO ORGINIZATION don't think I'm sayng they are not worthy I'M JUST GIVING A OVERVIEW!  Thank you CLiff for having this web page as well!||||reg||1
Z||000063||tgowens||12-15-2001||05:20 PM||||FIEROJOHN-WI, I agree with most of your views. It is my goal as a part of the leadership of CFOG-International to produce Fiero shows and events without the politics, without the confusion, and without the complications. While this may seem an impossible task, it CAN be done. Politicing within this organization is highly frowned on by me even though it may seem that, from some of these posts, that we are "posturing" -- not so, simply getting feedback from those who have attended shows and belonged to clubs as to their good, bad and ugly - then we try to implement polocies and procedures to see that the bad and ugly do not occur in CFOG-International.<P>It is our greatest desire to bring together the entire Fiero community with CFOG-International - we will have something for everyone since there is a wide diversity of Fiero owners and enthusiasts.<P>We hope to align with other Pontiac and GM organizations and may be showing at their shows and they may be showing in ours (kinda like a "corral" effect) - it may be that they will judge theirs and we will judge ours or we may both judge all, it remains to be seen but, we can try.<P>We recognize that there are many established events already popularized in this country, such as the Dells Run and Daytona. I have spoken to the people who operate The Daytona event and, although they are already members of CFOG-International, they prefer not to be sanctioned by any one organization. That is fine with me and we have discussed the possibility of assisting them with that event to make it even bigger and better and we will portray whatever role is necessary to see that is the case.<P>I would also like to invite you and your organizations to become a part of CFOG-International with the assurance that we will also support you, your club and your events in whatever manner is necessary to assure their growth and success. We can be an integral part of your growth and continuation just as you could ours -- it <I>should</I> be a two-way street...........<P>Ideally, we are all Fiero owners and Fiero lovers. There is no need to quarrel with each other over who does what to who and when - this is a big world and certainly big enough for us all.<P>As the Chairman of CFOG-International I pledge our support to you and make the same commitments to you that we do to our members, that we are accessible and accountable.<P>These posts are not meant to create any dissention with anyone nor to "flame" or "bash" any one or any organization. What the intention has been was to enlighten Fiero owners and enthusiasts, club members, former club members and future club members as to a group that has been created with these thoughts in mind. WE <I>are</I> different than other clubs because we dare to be different. We will strive to promote the Pontiac Fiero in whatever manner we are capable and in a positive light.<P>Thank you for your time and consideration. and yes, many thanks to Cliff Pennock, who, without his talents (and love of the Fiero) this Forum would not exist to allow us to provide our thoughts and ideas for each other.<P>Terry Owens<BR>Chairman, CFOG-International<p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 12-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000064||2M4 Dale||12-15-2001||06:13 PM||||AMEN Terry..........we want to bring in all facets of Fierodom......not just shows and show cars!!! I would like to see CFOG-I sponsered Driving events......Racing.....etc. We would like to try to cater to all of the diverse Fiero population.<P>2M4 Dale<BR>Central Region Director CFOG-I||||reg||4
Z||000065||dcaprio||12-15-2001||07:25 PM||||I've been watching this post for a while now and have a few questions.<BR>1st <BR>   What will the club do for me that I cannot get elsewhere ? I live in Pa. and there seem to be more shows and get togethers than any other area.<BR>2nd<BR>   Do you think its a good idea to have a founder of the club running a buisness selling parts to the Fiero community ? No offence to anyone . <BR>3rd<BR>    Will other Fiero based businesses be willing to give discounts to members with the involvement in the leadership of this club  by a competitor? <BR>4th<BR>    You said a club run by members if so please explain how and when election will be held ? Will a member have the same chance at getting elected from Pa. as from California ?<BR>    Don't get me wrong I would love to see an international club do well. I just don't think it could be done. <BR>     It might be worth my $25.00 just to see what happens in the next few months. Good luck and if things work out I might join but for now I'll have to wait till I see if the club is just a dream or what many of us have been waiting for. <BR>    ||||reg||1
Z||000066||tgowens||12-15-2001||07:58 PM||||dcaprio, let me see if I can answer your questions........<P>1. "What will the club do for me that I cannot get elsewhere ? I live in Pa. and there seem to be more shows and get togethers than any other area." - <P>This may be true but, keep in mind that those are usually local shows hosted by local clubs. We will assist in any local/regional show for any of our member clubs upon their invitation. As for what the club will do for you that you cannot get elsewhere - you will have to be the judge of that. We have been stating benefits on this post and also on our web site and the list of benefits will continue to grow - all in all it is quite a bit for slightly more than $2 per month.<P>2. "Do you think its a good idea to have a founder of the club running a buisness selling parts to the Fiero community ? No offence to anyone ." - <P>I am the founder of this organization and I have a graphics & design business. I do sell Fiero parts of my own but I do not run a business selling parts to the Fiero community. I am not really certain what you are referring to here.<P>3. "Will other Fiero based businesses be willing to give discounts to members with the<BR>involvement in the leadership of this club by a competitor?" - <P>See the above statement. As for other Fiero based business giving discounts it is entirely up to that particular business. We may, at some point in time, have a bi-lateral involvement with certain businesses who, at their discretion, may offer discounts to members.<P>4. "You said a club run by members if so please explain how and when election will be held ? Will a member have the same chance at getting elected from Pa. as from California? Don't get me wrong I would love to see an international club do well. I just don't think it could be done." - <P>CFOG-International is run by its members inasmuch as each member has an accessability to all levels of the management of the organization. I, myself, take a personal involvement in the club even though we have an operations group consisting of Officer's and a Board of Directors. Officers and Directors are nominated and elected to serve two year terms (with overlapping years). At the time for nominations, ballots will be sent to all members. Yes, a member from PA will have just as much of an oppertunity of being nominated and elected as one from CA - If you will notice our structure, we are divided into regions, each with a Regional Director and Deputy Directors. All are Board members and participate in monthly online meetings. As elections are held this will create openings within those regions. In addition, there will be the need for committee members, etc. per region meaning this - we want all of our members to have a part in the organization if they wish. It is with the general memberships thoughts, ideas, and concerns that will guide this club. We have to be responsive to the membership to address those thoughts, ideas and concerns.<P>I assure you, this organization is not a dream! It was created from thoughts, ideas and concerns of many of us who, for one reason or another, wanted something different, something better, something more aggressive and progressive and something worth $25 a year.............I am not just the Chairman nor are the Officers and Directors just Officers and Directors, we are Fiero owners, enthusiasts, finatics, nuts - whatever you want to call us but we all share the same common goal - the enjoyment and preservation of the Fiero.<p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 12-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000067||dcaprio||12-15-2001||08:54 PM||||Tjowens<BR>   I was referring to George Miller of the Fiero shop. As no elections have been held he must be a founding member. I was trying to avoid a flame war by not mentioning his name.<BR>   As for a dream, You spoke of everything your going to do but have yet to fulfill. as the quote goes "The best made plans of mice and men often wander and go a stray". Only time will tell if your club will be all you hope it can be. Remember actions speak loader than words.<BR>   I wish you the best and I hope you prove all of us doubters wrong.||||reg||1
Z||000068||tgowens||12-15-2001||09:46 PM||||dcaprio, I understand now. In actuality, Mr. Miller was appointed by me to serve a first term until such time that elections were held. At this point in time Mr. Miller is no longer associated with CFOG-International as either an Officer or a Director. He is, however, one of the general membership.<P>Yes, I do know that actions speak louder than words - that is what this topic has been all about - actions of a club to fulfill its promises to its membership. This one certainly will .. the members expect it, the members deserve it, and the members shall have it - it's really very simple - meeting expectations and fulfilling promises. ||||reg||1
Z||000069||SpaceKyoti||12-15-2001||10:30 PM||||I've also been reading all this baloney about the Fiero Festival in Williamsburg: "Phil didn't do this for us, Curry didn't do that for us," etc., etc., etc.  Well, I talked to Phil and Curry, they were very helpful and friendly, and found a way to take care of my every need. They told me that the port-a-john people didn't bring the toilets to the car show like they were supposed to, but some Fiero people were blaming Phil and Curry instead. Sure seems to me that some Fiero people are more interested in pointing the finger at somebody or finding something wrong with something than they are in the reason for coming together in the first place. In case they forgot, that reason was to have a good time! I had a great time, and I didn't mind walking across the street to use the bathroom. For crimony sakes, what about those other Festivals I went to, where there weren't any restrooms at all??? I don't remember hearing or reading this kind of baloney about lack of bathrooms! Besides, what's the big deal about bathrooms? Was this a national bathroom festival?? And if you really need to use one during the show, and one is available (even if it's across the street), why raise a big stink about it? Just go there and "do your business" and come back, you won't miss anything. What the heck's the matter with these people? Is their skin so thin that they have to have everything done for them? It's time for them to grow up! I say Curry and all the members of the FOCSEVA did a fantastic job of organizing this year's Fiero Festival in Williamsburg, and I really hope there will be another one there! All the FOCSEVA members were friendly and helpful, and they worked real hard to do everything they could to help, and they were happy to do it. From what I heard, many of them had never even been to a national Fiero Festival before, and they spent as much time working at this one as they did enjoying the good company of their fellow Fiero owners from around the country. We need more people like them. Who cares what who said to who at the car show? Talk is cheap. Actions speak a lot more to me, and these FOCSEVA people really did a bang up job! For me, this was the best Fiero Festival I ever went to, and the lack of port-a-johns at the car show was as insignificant as those Bozos who raised the stink about it.||||reg||6
Z||000070||tgowens||12-16-2001||12:02 AM||||SpaceKyoti, This isn't about port-a-pottys but you did get one very good point across -- I agree that Curry and FOCSEVA did an excellent job, right down to hosting the show information on their own web site -- and they are to be commended for doing what they could, but -- here is the point -- it was a NATIONAL show - why should Curry and FOCSEVA have had to do all of the work? And, these issues aren't about just that one National Festival but rather about things that just shouldn't be. ||||reg||1
Z||000071||SpaceKyoti||12-16-2001||11:29 AM||||I forgot to mention that when I was talking to Fiero Phil at the Festival, I asked him how he got everything organized in Williamsburg while he was 3000 miles away at FOCOA headquarters? He told me that Curry volunteered (there's that word again) to organize whatever Phil and Gloria asked for, to make things easier for them. He also said that Curry even asked him to have all the door prizes and free giveaways shipped to HIS house so Phil and Gloria wouldn't have to haul them clear across the country. Turns out that Curry showed up at the festival in his pickup truck, with a whole truckload of Fiero Festival stuff. Just to set the record straight, according to Phil, Curry and his crew didn't have to do all the work. They all volunteered - all of them. They wanted to do whatever they could to make the national Fiero Festival the best that they could. These FOCSEVA people are great, and they love their club and FOCOA. Kind of makes me wish I had done more, myself. Maybe in this day and age we could all take a lesson from them, a lesson that teaches that we should give more (in service to others).||||reg||1
Z||000072||tgowens||12-16-2001||11:45 AM||||I agree, it's all too often that ones look at the things that are wrong rather than the things that are right. Unfortunately, it is the things that are wrong that people tend to remember the most. <p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 12-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000073||FieroBUZZ||12-16-2001||01:56 PM||||I've started to reply several times, but never quite managed to get my thoughts straight.  Obviously, this club (or any) will appeal to some and not to others. I don't see anything wrong with that.<P>As an outsider, and a foreigner to boot, I don't get the opportunity to attend all the US events.  But I do see very strong personalities at both ends of the dance hall who will never meet in the middle. That is the nature of things, and usually no one is absolutely right. Just remember that a public p*ssing match really doesn't help anyone.<P>I don't see any advantage to join from my perspective, but wish CFOG-I all the best.<BR>Gary||||reg||1
Z||000074||2M4 Dale||12-16-2001||11:06 PM||||As a Regional Director for CFOG-I I would like to say that I did not get into this thing to have a pissing contest with anyone...I want to be part of a concerted effort to build a better mouse-trap. I feel as if we are on the right track and that we will succeed in our efforts. I would also like to say that it is not nor never was our intent to go to war with FOCOA........We do need members to succeed and we will do what we need to do to get them.........no-one at CFOG-I expects FOCOA to turn over and die just cause we exist.....it is a big world fellers.....lol. <P>Oh, and by the way, I still have friends that are members of FOCOA.....I won't cross them off my Christmas card list just cause they like FOCOA......we can all be and should be friends........remember our central theme ...     PONTIAC FIERO  !!!||||reg||12
Z||000075||FieroHarry||12-16-2001||11:45 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by tgowens:<BR><B> -- I agree that Curry and FOCSEVA did an excellent job, right down to hosting the show information on their own web site -- and they are to be commended for doing what they could </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Just wanted to say thanks to those who can understand just how hard we here in VA worked to try and make this show a nice one.<BR>I will pass along the good remarks to my fellow FOCOSEVA members without computers <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>CFOG-I,<BR>I hope you can make this work, not for myself but for the fellow Fiero owners that will join your club.<BR>I will take no sides, nor throw flames.<P>Terry,<BR>One question though,<P>What does one do when 2 days before the National event,<P> (while every one is driving across country and cant be reached)<P>do when the place you picked cancels out and says no?<P>Answer: Try your very best, then stand back and hear all the complaints..........<P>Just like us  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Nuff said, Merry Christmas to all!<P><P>------------------<BR><BR><B>"If you want to blend in, take the bus!"</B>||||reg||9
Z||000076||tgowens||12-17-2001||12:33 AM||||Harry, I know how difficult things like that can be. {putting together a show or festival takes a lot of work and it seems like it is always just a handful of people wo accomplish it. The festivals and shows that I have worked on (this goes back many many years in event planning) we always made a contingency plan for emergencies. Sometimes it was the plan B that got used at the last minute.<P>The answer to your question is: enact plan B.<P>Nevertheless, it is a thankless job without much reward at times. <P>As I have said, I would like nothing better than to see all of the Fiero factions come together for the good of the Fiero community. No, this is not a "flame war" or a "pissing match" - we (CFOG-International) just had ideas of what we wanted to do - it was presented to FOCOA and they turned it down so we are doing it, that's all it's about.<P>As I have said before, people can be members of both clubs, either club or neither club - whatever suits them. It's a big world and There should be something for everyone. That's why we have stressed over and over again that we are an "alternative" not a replacement.<P>And, by the way, it was great meeting you in Williamsburg.....||||reg||1
Z||000077||West Coast Fiero||12-17-2001||01:05 AM||||<FONT COLOR="red"><B>SUGGESTION</B></FONT><P> give up on attempting to host a show that is nearly the same time and date as the FOCOA Western Regional...<P><FONT COLOR="blue"><B>INSTEAD</B></FONT><P>Host the Fiero Power Tour that every Pennock's member wants so badly to happen<P>click this link for details...   <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/010381.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/010381.html</A> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif">  <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<p>[This message has been edited by West Coast Fiero (edited 12-17-2001).]||||reg||1
